*********** Record types ********* 
immutable data- value can't be changed over time. Ex- value type and string
mutable- value can be changed. Ex- reference type

"record" type is basically used to create an immutable object. It's properties behave as readonly.
Ex:- <"init;" used instead of "set;">
public record Person
{
    public string LastName { get; init; } = default!
    public string FirstName { get; init; } = default!

    public Person(string first, string last) => (FirstName, LastName) = (first, last);
}

is equivalent to

public class Person
{
    public readonly string LastName { get; }
    public readonly string FirstName { get; }

    public Person(string first, string last) => (FirstName, LastName) = (first, last);
}

However, record type can be used to create muttable object as well (same as class).

public record Person
{
    public string FirstName { get; set; } = default!;
    public string LastName { get; set; } = default!;
};

** It can be used as Positional syntax for property definition if the obejct is defined as record (not class).
		EX- Person p = new ("First Name", "Last Name");
** Value equality for record type 	- if we compare two record objects, if both of them has same set of properties and their values , then equal operator would consider them as identical.

** An immutable property of a record instance can be made mutable by using a "with" expression. The result of a with expression is a shallow copy of original object.
Ex- 
public record Person(string FirstName, string LastName)
{
    public string[]   { get; init; } //imuutable property as "init" is defined instead of "set"
}
Person person1 = new("Nancy", "Davolio") { PhoneNumbers = new string[1] };
person2 = person1 with { PhoneNumbers = new string[1] }; //PhoneNumbers property is set as muttable.
 
** "record" type is similar to type class. It supports inheritance. But Neither record can inherit a class nor a class can inherit a record.
public abstract record Person(string FirstName, string LastName);
public record Teacher(string FirstName, string LastName, int Grade)
    : Person(FirstName, LastName);
public record Student(string FirstName, string LastName, int Grade)
    : Person(FirstName, LastName);

**"record" type can be sealed too.
public sealed record Student : Person
{
}

